<!--
  Klinik.Net lockup, single colour, converted from prototype/assets/logo-mono.svg.

  ## Why the tile is a stroke rather than a fill

  The SVG fills the tile with currentColor and strokes the glyph with a CSS
  custom property that reverses the two, falling back to #FFFFFF. That
  property cannot cross an img boundary; the SVG's own comment acknowledges
  this and leans on the white fallback. VectorDrawable has no custom
  properties and no currentColor, so a literal translation would hardcode a
  two-colour mark that only works on the default ink tile.

  A single-colour asset is what "mono" means, and one colour is what a tint at
  the call site can recolour per theme. So the tile is drawn as an outline and
  the glyph as strokes, both in the drawable's own ink: one Image with a
  ColorFilter.tint gives a correct lockup on the light rail, the dark rail, or
  the coral splash.

  The wordmark paths are carried across verbatim; every SVG transform became an
  equivalent group, as in logo.xml.
-->
